# Captured third-party tool output used as test fixtures.
#
# These files are byte-exact recordings of what a real tool emitted, parsed by
# the wrappers in src/worker/tools/ and src/lib/scanners/. Git must never
# convert their line endings in either direction: a parser that splits on "\n"
# sees a trailing "\r" and silently fails to match.
#
# Why this file exists. core.autocrlf=true is set on this repo and there was no
# .gitattributes at all, so git checked every text fixture out as CRLF on
# Windows while every committed blob is LF. sqlmap's host-log parser splits on
# "\n" only, so "reads the same point from the per-host log file"
# (sqlmapOutput.test.ts) and "finds the injection point from the host log alone
# when console output was lost" (sqlmap.test.ts) failed on any Windows checkout
# and passed in CI, where Linux checks out LF. A Windows contributor therefore
# could not trust a local `npm run test:unit`.
#
# The failure was selective, which is what made it confusing: git's own binary
# detection already left clean.stdout.txt, unreachable.stdout.txt and
# vulnerable.stdout.txt untouched, so the tests reading console output passed
# while the two reading the host log did not.
#
# -text rather than eol=lf on purpose. These are recordings, not source: if a
# tool one day emits real CRLF (raw HTTP headers, say), eol=lf would rewrite it
# on commit and the fixture would stop being a faithful capture. -text pins the
# bytes both ways.
#
# Scope is deliberately the captured-output trees only. The rest of the repo
# keeps its CRLF working-tree convention (see CLAUDE.md rule 3), and the source
# files that happen to live under a fixtures/ directory — bench/fixtures/sites.ts
# and tests/fixtures/test-app/ — are ordinary code, not recordings, so they are
# left to follow that convention.
hebridescyber-app/src/worker/tools/fixtures/** -text
hebridescyber-app/src/lib/scanners/fixtures/** -text
hebridescyber-app/bench/fixtures/retire-subset.json -text
